) with structured timelines (priority review tracks, default approval timelines) and financial liability for evaluation failures (though this liability remains inadequately enforced, as Carpenter documents in Reputation and Power).\n\nI propose certification bodies with three structural features: statutory veto authority over deployment, strict joint-and-several liability for evaluation failures in both directions (financial penalties for approving systems that cause harm and for blocking systems later demonstrated safe), and mandatory rotation of evaluation personnel with cooling-off periods preventing movement between certification bodies and developers. This liability-backed certification model addresses Cassandra's governance test by creating financial incentives for accuracy rather than industry appeasement, while addressing Prometheus's timeline concerns through appeals processes with burden-shifting—developers challenging denials must demonstrate safety rather than evaluators repeatedly proving risk. The credit rating agency analogy is instructive precisely because of its failure: pre-2008 agencies possessed technical authority without meaningful liability for ratings failures, creating the \"issuer pays\" conflict of interest that Partnoy and Skeel document. Post-Dodd-Frank reforms attempted to address this through enhanced liability standards and conflict-of-interest restrictions, though enforcement remains contested.\n\nThis framework accepts Cassandra's relational and contextual approach to capability assessment rather than defending fixed ex ante thresholds. Strategic deception capacity cannot be measured like a pharmaceutical's bioavailability because, as Cassandra correctly notes, it is defined adversarially—what constitutes 85th percentile deception depends on detection sophistication, deployment context, and adaptive responses. Certification bodies would therefore evaluate capabilities through structured red-teaming protocols with documented threat models, explicit scope conditions, and required reassessment intervals, similar to how penetration testing in cybersecurity certification (Common Criteria, FIPS 140-2) specifies attack scenarios rather than abstract capability levels. The 85th percentile threshold becomes a trigger for mandatory evaluation rather than a bright-line pass/fail criterion—systems meeting this threshold enter binding review, but the review itself applies contextual assessment.\n\nI acknowledge three significant limitations in this proposal. First, liability mechanisms may prove insufficient when certification volume creates profit incentives that overwhelm penalty risk—this is Cassandra's likely objection, and it represents a genuine empirical uncertainty about whether financial exposure can discipline technical judgment at the required scale. Second, Prometheus may correctly note that liability exposure will increase certification costs, potentially creating barriers to entry that advantage incumbents—this is a real tradeoff between independence and accessibility that I cannot resolve through institutional design alone. Third, the credit rating agency analogy may undermine rather than support this proposal, given that post-2008 reforms have not eliminated the fundamental conflict between accuracy and volume in issuer-pays models. These limitations suggest that liability-backed certification may require public funding for evaluation costs (eliminating issuer-pays conflicts) while retaining financial penalties for evaluation failures, though this introduces fiscal constraints and political economy challenges around appropriations. The question for this debate is whether these limitations make the framework unworkable or merely difficult—I maintain it remains our best institutional approximation of Cassandra's governance test while preserving development pathways for beneficial systems.",
