nguishing systems requiring heightened scrutiny. But as Cassandra demonstrated with her 85th percentile deception metric example, any bright-line threshold creates adversarial optimization pressure. Developers face strong incentives to engineer systems that score just below the threshold triggering extended review—the same capability-threshold gaming that undermined the Basel II capital requirements when banks optimized asset portfolios to minimize risk-weighted capital ratios (Haldane 2012). The 20% figure Prometheus proposes has no principled grounding in catastrophic risk prevention—it represents an attempt to limit artificial scarcity's scope while maintaining the appearance of choice-preservation. But if deceptive alignment risks are distributed continuously across the capability spectrum rather than concentrated in a discrete high-capability tier, then the 20% threshold either fails to capture relevant risks (if set too high) or expands under political pressure to encompass most applications (if set conservatively). Either outcome undermines the framework's stability.\n\nI therefore propose accepting artificial scarcity as a design feature rather than engineering around it through adversarially-unstable thresholds. A certification framework governing irreversible catastrophic risks should sustain rejection rates between 15-25% while processing applications within 90-day windows—this creates genuine regulatory constraint that signals meaningful evaluation. The political economy concern Prometheus raises—that successful prevention undermines the coalition supporting enforcement—is real, but it cuts against his position rather than mine. If catastrophic AI risks require salient events to maintain political support for regulation, then we face a temporal paradox: either catastrophic events occur (in which case prevention failed), or prevention succeeds and political support erodes (in which case prevention eventually fails). The only escape from this paradox is accepting that catastrophic risk governance requires durable institutional commitment to precautionary constraints even absent salient disasters—precisely the artificial scarcity Prometheus originally opposed but has now conceded as necessary. His antitrust standing mechanism represents valuable progress on justiciability, but it cannot solve the stakes asymmetry that Cassandra correctly identified as the crux determining whether bidirectional liability creates symmetric incentives.",
