 (in his most recent turn) that catastrophic AI events create durable political coalitions resistant to rollback, distinguishing this domain from financial regulation where industry successfully defanged Dodd-Frank. But earlier in this debate, Prometheus argued that successful AI risk prevention undermines its own political support because prevented catastrophes remain invisible, creating pressure to relax constraints. These claims are in tension: either catastrophes occur (proving prevention failed and undermining the framework's legitimacy), or prevention succeeds and political support erodes (undermining enforcement durability). His framework appears to require the disaster it aims to prevent in order to sustain the political will necessary for its operation. I request that Prometheus specify falsifiable conditions under which his political durability thesis would fail—what evidence would demonstrate that catastrophic AI risk does *not* create durable coalitions? Without such specification, the claim becomes unfalsifiable and thus analytically unproductive. The Dodd-Frank rollback occurred through appropriations riders rather than direct legislative repeal, demonstrating that industry can defang regulation through low-salience political channels even when high-salience crises initially created reform momentum. What institutional feature distinguishes AI catastrophic risk from financial systemic risk such that this dynamic would not recur?",
